// The media family registry (ADR-0010 §2, ADR-0013 §4; read by rv/world/media_family.hpp, which documents the format):
// how the games of each family stand on a shelf, in millimetres. An item's size is its manifest's scaleMeters, the
// 'cartridge' prefab at the family's size with its label on its front and its spine label on its top face; spine-out
// an item stands on its end with its top face outward (its width tall, its height deep), face-out it shows its front.
// The gaps and margins are the shelf prefab's; the headroom is the free height kept above an item in its level, so
// that it can be pulled out (ADR-0012 §3). The label is the item's label quad (its front less the label margin), what
// a cover is fitted to before its one resample into the 256 x 188 front layer (ADR-0013 §4); cartridge_home_16bit
// keeps I3's fit to the layer itself (coverFit "layer", ADR-0013 §4's stated exception: no SNES pixel moves).
//
// cartridge_home_16bit (I3): 12.5 x 8.5 x 2.0 cm, a 2.0 x 12.5 cm spine 8.5 cm deep at a 2.4 cm pitch; the template's
// own fixtures. The families of I4 step 2 are functional (ADR-0013 §4: realistic generic sizes, no real model; the rare
// formats take the nearest), each with its own fixtures at its slot sizes (assets/manifests/prefabs, hand-computed:
// the 16-bit fixtures' widths, boards, gaps and margins, the most levels whose compartments hold the item and its
// headroom, the hero band on the level nearest the eye):
//   cartridge_home_8bit   12.0 x 13.3 x 2.0 cm   home cartridges of generations 2 and 3
//   cartridge_home_64bit  11.6 x 7.6 x 1.8 cm    home cartridges of generation 5 and later
//   cartridge_handheld    5.7 x 6.5 x 0.8 cm     handheld cartridges (and the disk cards)
//   case_handheld         12.5 x 14.0 x 1.4 cm   handheld game cases (cards and small discs)
//   disc_case_cd          14.2 x 12.5 x 1.0 cm   CD jewel cases, a 12 x 12 cm insert
//   disc_case_dvd         13.5 x 19.0 x 1.4 cm   DVD and Blu-ray keep cases
//   big_box               19.0 x 24.0 x 4.5 cm   the 16- and 32-bit computers' and the PCs' boxes
//   generic_case          13.0 x 18.0 x 1.5 cm   everything else: the arcade room, the shared rooms, the 8-bit
//                                                computers, the other kinds
// Family ids are permanent: a family is never removed or redefined, a new definition takes a new id.
//
// I6 step 4 (ADR-0014 §2): cases and boxes stand on their side, upright, their spine out ("spineFacing": "side", the
// item turned a quarter about +Y, its -X face outward with an upright spine label: a spine-out slot of the item's depth
// wide, its height tall and its width deep), as on real CD, DVD and box shelves. Their families are new ids, each naming
// the one it succeeds ("succeeds": a stored room of that family is regenerated once with its successor, a new room takes
// the successor); their modules are their item's width deep, with the most levels whose compartments hold the item
// upright and its headroom (the hero band on the level nearest the eye), and their display fixtures, face-out, are
// their predecessors':
//   disc_case_cd_v2       8 levels x 56, band level 5 (disc_case_cd: 7 x 56)
//   disc_case_dvd_v2      5 levels x 44, band level 3 (disc_case_dvd: 7 x 44)
//   case_handheld_v2      7 levels x 44, band level 4 (case_handheld: 8 x 44)
//   big_box_v2            4 levels x 16, band level 2 (big_box: 5 x 16)
//   generic_case_v2       6 levels x 41, band level 4 (generic_case: 8 x 41)
// Their spine labels cover the flat part of the face, top to bottom ("spineLabelWidthMm", "spineLabelHeightMm": the
// manifest's spineLabelSize; the big box's widened to its face's flat part), a printed spine of the title on the cover's
// colour drawn at that shape, so that a shelf reads as a rack of coloured spines (example.png, panels 2 and 4); their
// shells are dark ("shellMaterial": the jewel case's smoky plastic, the keep case's black, the other cases' dark
// plastic, the big box's cardboard), the cartridges' the theme's plastic.
// generic_case_v2 succeeds generic_case in console and shared rooms only ("succeedsIn"; ADR-0014 §2, amended on
// 2026-10-24): the arcade room kept generic_case until I6 step 6's arcade_cabinet succeeded it there.
// The cartridge families keep I3's spine on the top face and have no successor.
//
// I6 step 6 (ADR-0014 §2 and §6): arcade_cabinet, the arcade room's family, succeeds generic_case in the arcade room
// only (its stored room migrated once, as the cases and boxes were). Its item is a cabinet's screen, 30 x 40 x 0.6 cm
// (a vertical monitor's portrait screen, the cover fitted to its 298 x 398 mm label, drawn through a picture tube:
// "labelMaterial"), which never stands spine-out ("spineFacing": "front": its spine-out slot is its face-out one); its
// every fixture is a pair of generic upright cabinets (furniture.arcade_cabinet.generic, 0.88 x 1.7 x 0.4 m, one
// screen each), so that the arcade's modules are rows of cabinets along its walls and the display sockets pairs
// facing its aisle; a hero-wall or side-wall module's screens are the spine section's (the titles in the fill order,
// the reserve kept, no hero band), the display fixtures' the featured titles'. Capacity (the reserve kept): 6 featured,
// 4 on the hero wall, 2 per side-wall module: 33 titles in the 9 x 10 m room, 40 at 12 m, 53 at 16 m.
